SignedChange 2.0 — Strategic Design Brief & Redesign Plan
Your decisions establish a clear direction:
* Brand: Premium
* Priority audience: Balanced — contractors/freelancers and employers/clients
* Scope: Complete redesign
* Logo: Evolve the existing identity, not replace it
My recommendation is to position SignedChange 2.0 as a premium agreement-change management platform: a product that helps two parties document changes, approve them, sign them, and retain a reliable record.
This is more ambitious than presenting SignedChange as a tool that simply generates signed PDFs. The PDF is the output; the real product is the trusted process that gets both parties to agree.
1. Strategic product positioning
The problem we own
Agreements change after work has started.
* A contractor receives a request for additional work.
* A freelancer agrees to deliver more than originally scoped.
* An employer and employee agree to new working conditions.
* A client and service provider agree to a price or schedule adjustment.
These changes are often communicated through conversations, WhatsApp messages, phone calls, and emails. Later, the parties may disagree about what was actually approved.
SignedChange turns those changes into clear, signed, retrievable records.
Recommended positioning statement
Recommended positioning
When agreements change, make it official.
Document the change. Get both sides’ approval. Keep a record everyone can refer to.
Supporting message: From extra work and revised prices to new deadlines and responsibilities, SignedChange helps both parties put agreed changes in writing.
Brand personality
Attribute	What it should communicate
Professional	Suitable for business use
Trustworthy	Careful with agreements and evidence
Precise	Clear terms, amounts, dates, and approvals
Modern	Simple, digital, mobile-first workflows
Approachable	Both parties can use it without technical knowledge
Premium should not mean complicated, expensive-looking, or intimidating. It should mean clarity, confidence, consistency, and attention to detail.
2. Audience strategy: two sides of the same transaction
You selected a balanced audience. I agree, but I would avoid designing two separate products.
Both audiences have the same fundamental need: record a change that affects an existing agreement and obtain the other party’s approval.
Contractors and freelancers
* Additional work and scope changes
* Revised prices and payment terms
* New deadlines and delivery dates
* Client approvals and project records
Employers, clients and service businesses
* Revised duties and responsibilities
* Changes to working hours
* Revised service arrangements
* Written approval of operational changes
Important product decision: The person creating a change and the person approving it should have equally clear experiences. The invited party must not feel like a secondary user or be forced to create an account just to review and sign.
3. Visual identity and design system
A. Logo evolution
Keep the recognizable foundation.
Your current identity has useful elements: the SC monogram, document-inspired geometry, approval checkmark, and blue-and-orange contrast.
The problem is not the concept. It is the visual complexity and how the mark scales down.
My direction:
* Preserve the SC recognition.
* Simplify the folded-document and checkmark elements.
* Remove unnecessary gradients, shadows, and decorative details.
* Create a strong flat-color version.
* Develop separate icon, horizontal, and stacked logo variations.
* Ensure the mark works at favicon size, on mobile navigation, and in PDF headers.
The logo should be recognizable in one color before we add color.
B. Proposed color system
Core palette
Deep navy
#123B63 · Authority and trust
Business blue
#1766A5 · Primary actions and links
Warm amber
#F2A900 · Accent and key actions
Soft neutral
#F5F7FA · Backgrounds and surfaces
White
#FFFFFF · Content surfaces
Charcoal
#202B38 · Primary text
These are proposed starting values, not final brand specifications. We should validate contrast and consistency before implementation.
The existing blue and orange identity should remain recognizable, but the overall interface should rely more on navy, white, and neutral surfaces. Amber should be an accent, not the background for every important element.
C. Typography
Use a clean, modern sans-serif system.
My starting recommendation:
* Headings: Manrope or Inter.
* Body: Inter.
* Numbers and amounts: consistent, highly legible numeric styling.
* Document output: a more formal typographic hierarchy suitable for printed agreements.
Use a limited type scale and avoid the current mixture of very small text, dense form labels, and oversized mobile headings.
D. Component design
Create a consistent component library:
* Primary, secondary, and text buttons
* Form fields and validation states
* Cards and pricing panels
* Status indicators
* Signing and consent panels
* Dialogs and confirmation screens
* Navigation and footer
* Empty states, errors, and success messages
The same components should be used across the marketing website and application wherever appropriate.
