* Give pricing a more deliberate comparison, including clear limits and benefits.
* Make the FAQ and footer less visually dense.
* Keep the free tools as a distinct acquisition channel, with links back to the relevant SignedChange workflow.
5. Application redesign: the most important part
The screenshots show that the current application puts many fields and decisions on one long page. It works as a functional form, but it does not yet feel like a premium guided product.
The mobile form is particularly important: users must enter personal details, party details, work descriptions, money, dates, signing preferences, and signatures in one continuous experience.
I recommend replacing this with a guided change-record creation workflow.
Proposed five-step experience
Create a change record
New experience
Step 1 of 5 · Change details
What has changed?
Describe the change to your existing agreement.
Change type
Additional work
Description
Additional installation work requested by the client…
This is an illustrative concept, not a live form.
* Change details Change type, description, existing agreement reference, and template. 
* People involved Creator, other party, roles, and contact details. 
* Financial and schedule impact Amount, currency, tax, payment changes, and relevant dates. 
* Review and confirm Present the complete proposed change in readable language before submission. 
* Sign and share Capture the creator’s consent, invite the other party, and track signing. 
UX rules
* Ask only for information required at that point.
* Use conditional fields: for example, show payment-change fields only when relevant.
* Save drafts safely so users do not lose their work.
* Explain why contact information is needed.
* Display the other party’s signing options clearly.
* Give both parties a readable review screen before signing.
* Make the mobile experience the primary design constraint.
* Provide clear progress, validation, loading, error, and success states.
Separate the two signing experiences
Creator experience: prepare the change, review the terms, sign, and send an invitation.
Invited party experience: open a secure link, inspect the proposed change, understand the effect, accept or decline, and sign where appropriate.
The invited party should not be forced to navigate the creator’s dashboard or see account upselling before reviewing the agreement.
6. Pricing and monetization redesign
I would retain your current three-tier structure during the redesign, but make the commercial logic more deliberate.
Plan	Current offer	Strategic role
Free	3 documents	Acquisition and product trial
Pro Monthly	$9/month	Recurring revenue
Pro Lifetime	$49 once	One-time purchase
My concerns
* Lifetime pricing can create long-term hosting, maintenance, support, and infrastructure obligations without recurring revenue.
* The monthly plan needs benefits that customers value after creating their first few records.
* The free plan must demonstrate the product’s complete core workflow rather than offering an incomplete experience.
* The difference between free and paid must be immediately understandable.
Recommended premium features to validate
* Saved templates and reusable parties
* Searchable document history
* Organization branding
* Signing reminders
* Better record management and exports
* Multiple projects or client workspaces
* Team access and collaboration, if demand supports it
Do not build all these immediately. First measure which features drive repeat usage and upgrades.
Also, make sure payment verification and entitlement activation happen on the backend. A successful-looking checkout redirect must not, by itself, grant Pro access.
7. Product trust, security and engineering requirements
A premium visual redesign cannot compensate for a weak trust model.
The following should be design requirements and engineering requirements, not optional finishing touches.
Area	Required outcome
Privacy	Clear explanation of what is stored and who can access it
Signing	Evidence of the exact terms each party approved
Link security	Unpredictable, scoped, revocable, expiring signing links
Authorization	Only authorized users can access or modify records
Integrity	Finalized documents cannot be silently modified
Audit history	Important creation, invitation, review, signing, and finalization events are recorded
PDF	Consistent, readable, printable document output
Billing	Verified payment events and correct entitlements
Recovery	Clear and safe account/device recovery process
Accessibility	Keyboard access, sufficient contrast, readable labels, and clear errors
One distinction is especially important: a signature image or typed name is not automatically proof of a qualified electronic signature or guaranteed legal enforceability. SignedChange should describe its actual consent and evidence process precisely, and obtain appropriate legal review for the jurisdictions in which it operates.
8. Redesign implementation roadmap
I recommend six phases. Grokbot should work from approved specifications, not make independent architectural assumptions.
* Phase 1 — Product and technical discovery Start here Before changing anything, Grokbot should inspect:
    * Existing repository structure and framework.
    * Backend APIs and frontend routes.
    * Database schema and relationships.
    * Authentication and signing workflows.
    * PDF generation and storage.
    * Payment processing and entitlement logic.
    * Existing tests and deployment configuration.
* Deliverable: Existing-system architecture report, risk register, and proposed target architecture. Exit condition: We understand what must be preserved, improved, replaced, or newly developed. 
* Phase 2 — Product architecture and UX specification Define the application workflows, page hierarchy, state transitions, data model changes, API contracts, and permissions. Deliverable: Approved SignedChange 2.0 product and technical specification. Exit condition: No major unresolved architectural decisions. 
* Phase 3 — Brand and design system Develop logo variations, colors, typography, spacing, responsive rules, and reusable interface components. Deliverable: SignedChange 2.0 design system and approved interface direction. Exit condition: Desktop and mobile visual direction approved. 
* Phase 4 — Website redesign Rebuild the homepage, navigation, pricing, solutions, resources, FAQs, and footer. Deliverable: Complete responsive marketing website. Exit condition: All navigation, links, forms, and calls to action work correctly. 
* Phase 5 — Application redesign Implement the guided record-creation workflow, party experiences, signing interfaces, dashboard, document history, and responsive states. Deliverable: Complete SignedChange 2.0 application experience. Exit condition: Existing signing, PDF, payment, and record-management functionality continues working. 
* Phase 6 — Security, testing and release Test the full lifecycle, permissions, signing links, PDF integrity, billing, mobile layouts, accessibility, and failure scenarios. Deliverable: Release candidate with documented test results and deployment/rollback plan. Exit condition: No unresolved critical security issues or critical workflow regressions. 
9. Non-negotiable rules for Grokbot
I would establish these engineering rules before implementation:
* Inspect before modifying. Never assume the existing architecture from the appearance of the website.
* Preserve working functionality. A redesign must not accidentally break signing, document generation, payments, or account recovery.
* No destructive database changes. No dropping tables, resetting production data, or destructive migrations without explicit approval and a verified recovery plan.
* Separate presentation from business logic. Do not duplicate payment, signing, or authorization rules in frontend code.
* Mobile-first and responsive. Test real mobile widths, not just desktop browser resizing.
* No fabricated features or trust claims. Only advertise capabilities that are implemented and verified.
* Test every critical workflow. A visually correct screen is not proof that the feature works.
* Small, reviewable milestones. Grokbot must report what it changed, why, what it tested, and what remains unresolved.
* Preserve existing work. Inspect Git status before changes and do not overwrite unrelated work or commit/push without authorization.
* Keep architectural decisions documented. Record important choices, alternatives, trade-offs, and consequences.
10. How we will measure success
The redesign should have measurable outcomes rather than simply looking more attractive.
Dimension	Success criteria
Brand	Consistent, recognizable identity across website, app, and PDFs
Usability	A first-time user can understand how to create and sign a record
Conversion	Visitors can move naturally from the homepage to record creation
Signing	Both parties can complete the intended signing workflow
Mobile	No broken layouts, unreadable labels, or difficult interactions
Trust	Users understand privacy, signing, and document integrity
Reliability	No critical regression in existing functionality
Commercial	Clear pricing and measurable free-to-paid conversion
We should instrument the product to measure:
* Homepage-to-create-record conversion.
* Started versus completed records.
* Invitations sent versus signatures completed.
* Average time to complete a record.
* PDF generation failures.
* Free-to-Pro conversion.
* Returning users and repeat document creation.
Do not collect unnecessary personal information just to measure these metrics.
11. My final strategic recommendation
I would make three decisions the foundation of SignedChange 2.0:
First: Build around the change-record lifecycle. The product should be designed around documenting, reviewing, approving, signing, and retrieving changes—not around a collection of forms and PDF utilities.
Second: Make trust a product feature. The experience should communicate exactly what each person is agreeing to, preserve the evidence, and make finalized records easy to retrieve.
Third: Make premium mean effortless. A customer should be able to understand the product, create a record, and invite another person without needing a tutorial.
The existing product has a useful foundation. I would evolve it rather than discard it. Your current logo recognition, color identity, free tools, pricing structure, and core signing workflow are assets worth evaluating before changing them.
What I want us to do next
Before Grokbot writes a single line for the redesign, I recommend completing Phase 1: Product and technical discovery. That will give us the actual architecture of the existing SignedChange system and allow me to make informed CTO-level decisions rather than designing around assumptions.
Once we have that report, we can approve the target architecture and then produce the precise implementation instructions for Grokbot.
